DTUJm_FAk3tf6E ], your window into the hottest AI convos from China=E2=80=
=99s knowledge platform.
AI progress is no longer just about benchmarks. It=E2=80=99s about control =
systems, productization, and business models. Whether it=E2=80=99s robots r=
edefining physical limits, game companies building generative engines, or m=
odel providers arguing over revenue legitimacy, the competition is becoming=
 multidimensional.
=F0=9F=A4=96 Unitree Robotics=EF=BD=9C10 m/s sprint pushes humanoids into h=
igh-dynamic running era
Explore the discussion: https://www.zhihu.com/question/2026580932996456466 =
[ https://link.sbstck.com/redirect/1353c7ae-9199-40c1-8cd9-faacd14fc248?j=
=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E ]
Zhihu contributor @huyoust:
What=E2=80=99s actually impressive here isn=E2=80=99t comparing to Usain Bo=
lt, but that robots have moved from walking, jumping, and flipping into hig=
h-dynamic running. This involves foot placement control, leg coordination, =
impact absorption, posture stabilization, and hardware durability under hig=
h-frequency shocks=E2=80=94each of these is far more meaningful than headli=
ne comparisons.
Compared to other humanoids: on Feb 2, 2026, Shanghai startup MirrorMe Tech=
nology unveiled Bolt, reaching 10 m/s on a treadmill. That=E2=80=99s easier=
 than running on a real track like Unitree, but considering differences in =
size, structure, and design, both approaches have their merits.
Zhihu contributor @=E6=9D=8E=E6=98=8E=E6=AE=8A:
The interesting part isn=E2=80=99t the speed itself.
This robot doesn=E2=80=99t even need a head module=E2=80=94it runs just fin=
e without it. Motion control and vision are fully decoupled: IMU sits in th=
e torso, force sensors are in the legs. Losing the head is just =E2=80=9Clo=
sing a screen.=E2=80=9D
This fundamentally differs from humans and shows how redundancy in robotics=
 is redefining what counts as a =E2=80=9Ccritical component.=E2=80=9D
=F0=9F=8E=AE miHoYo=EF=BD=9CLPM 1.0 model introduces staged rendering for f=
aster generation
Join the conversation: https://www.zhihu.com/question/2025980183140741432/a=
nswer/2026240663562331715 [ https://link.sbstck.com/redirect/6b3975de-1cd5-=
4c86-9385-f760ea9dfae4?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoO=
DTUJm_FAk3tf6E ]
Zhihu contributor @=E5=8C=97=E5=86=B0=E6=B4=8B=E9=B3=95=E9=B1=BC:
The paper shows they built on open-source models like Wan and Qwen, with he=
avy engineering innovation.
A key idea: two-stage rendering for speed.
Stage 1 outputs coarse structure quickly
Stage 2 refines details for realism
Frames are processed in parallel: while frame 1 refines, frame 2 generates =
structure, frame 3 begins outlining.
This pipeline overlaps computation and significantly reduces latency.
=F0=9F=A6=AA Alibaba=EF=BD=9CHappy Oyster world model enables real-time 720=
P generated environments
Read more here: https://www.zhihu.com/question/2028087311204705592 [ https:=
//link.sbstck.com/redirect/07af81ad-8a2a-4127-a65c-c1066bf4ae14?j=3DeyJ1Ijo=
iMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E ]
Zhihu contributor @=E5=BE=B7=E9=87=8C=E5=85=8B=E6=96=87:
With an invite, I tested it=E2=80=94there are quite a few surprises, especi=
ally real-time world generation at 720P.
You can enter predefined worlds or generate your own via prompts. Inside, e=
nvironments are dynamically generated and editable in real time.
Zhihu contributor @=E5=BE=AE=E5=8D=B7=E7=9A=84=E5=A4=A7=E7=99=BD:
Based on official claims, it uses a video/pixel-based generation approach, =
which is exciting.
After testing dozens of cases:
Product form is impressive
Smoothness is decent
But latency and reconstruction lag behind alternative approaches
Visual quality still trails standard video generation models
Overall: promising, but far from mature.
=F0=9F=92=AC OpenAI vs Anthropic=EF=BD=9CRevenue dispute signals shift from=
 tech race to business competition
Dive into the thread: https://www.zhihu.com/question/2027692500500969204 [ =
https://link.sbstck.com/redirect/26bab5eb-2641-4076-9413-f50cb7bbb95d?j=3De=
yJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E ]
Zhihu contributor @=E6=9D=B0=E6=8B=89=E5=BE=B7=E7=AC=94=E4=B8=8B=E7=9A=84=
=E7=94=B7=E4=BA=BA:
When tech companies stop competing on models and breakthroughs, and start a=
rguing over accounting methods and reported revenue, it usually means the t=
echnical gap is narrowing.
This is where competition turns into straightforward business warfare.
Zhihu contributor @=E7=8E=8B=E5=85=8B=E4=B8=B9:
At one level, this is about how annualized revenue is calculated=E2=80=94im=
portant for ranking in financial markets.
At another level, the current AI capital cycle inherently contains =E2=80=
=9Cinflation=E2=80=9D: projected revenue gets multiplied, while actual cost=
s keep rising.
Both can coexist. AI has massive long-term potential, but also massive shor=
t-term bubbles. Historically, transformative technologies always create lar=
ger bubbles, and inflated revenue is just one common mechanism.
